0295867919921875, 0.0034027099609375, -0.0299530029296875, -0.0162811279296875, 0.001674652099609375, 0.01043701171875, 0.0165863037109375, -0.0034389495849609375, -0.0010929107666015625, -0.0100555419921875, -0.0131072998046875, 0.01120758056640625, -0.0022716522216796875, 0.01593017578125, 0.0247039794921875, -0.032928466796875, -0.016571044921875, 0.0033016204833984375, 0.00826263427734375, -0.0258941650390625, 0.01343536376953125, 0.0270538330078125, 0.0094146728515625, 0.0259246826171875, -0.045166015625, -0.0010223388671875, -0.004665374755859375, -0.013153076171875, 0.036041259765625, -0.01654052734375, 0.0157318115234375, -0.044769287109375, 0.01149749755859375, -0.01922607421875, 0.00409698486328125, -0.0014324188232421875, 0.016754150390625, -0.0159454345703125, -0.0075225830078125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\n**1. Introduction and Background**\n\nThis short work presents a novel extension of the permanental process model to include fixed effects, addressing the need to model both structured random variation and known covariate effects within a unified framework. While models such as generalized additive models (GAMs) (Hastie & Tibshirani, 1990) and Cox processes with covariates (M\u00f8ller & Waagepetersen, 2004) also combine fixed effects and nonparametric components, our approach is distinct in its use of the permanental process\u2014a flexible class of point process models with unique mathematical properties, particularly in spatial statistics and genomics. Specifically, the permanental process differs from standard Cox processes in that the joint distribution of counts is governed by the permanent of a kernel matrix, rather than a determinant or product structure, leading to different clustering and over-dispersion properties (McCullagh & M\u00f8ller, 2006; Shirai & Takahashi, 2003). To our knowledge, prior work has not provided a rigorous RKHS-based formulation for the permanental process with fixed effects, nor clarified the limiting behavior under a diffuse prior or the precise role of the equivalent kernel in regularization and interpretation. This extension enables interpretable decomposition of intensity functions and supports principled inference in settings where both known covariates and complex residual structure are present.\n\nThe permanental process is a type of Cox process (doubly stochastic Poisson process) where the random intensity function is constructed such that the joint distribution of counts in disjoint sets is governed by the permanent of a kernel matrix. Formally, for a domain \\(\\mathcal{X}\\), the permanental process defines a random intensity \\(\\lambda(x)\\) such that, for any finite collection \\(\\{x_1, \\ldots, x_n\\}\\), the joint factorial moment measures are given by the permanent of the kernel evaluated at these points (see McCullagh & M\u00f8ller, 2006). This process is particularly attractive for modeling over-dispersion and clustering in point patterns, and its connection to RKHS theory enables flexible, nonparametric modeling of intensity surfaces.\n\n**2. Methodology**\n\n*2.1 Model Specification*\n\nWe extend the standard permanental process model by introducing a fixed effects term. Specifically, let \\( x \\in \\mathcal{X} \\) denote a location or feature vector, and let \\( z(x) \\in \\mathbb{R}^p \\) be a vector of known covariates at \\( x \\). The intensity function is modeled as\n\\[\n\\lambda(x) = \\exp\\left( z(x)^\\top \\beta + f(x) \\right),\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}^p \\) are fixed effects coefficients and \\( f \\) is a function in an RKHS \\( \\mathcal{H}_k \\) associated with a positive definite kernel \\( k \\).\n\nWe place a prior on \\( \\beta \\), typically a multivariate normal:\n\\[\n\\beta \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\tau^2 I_p),\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\tau^2 \\) is the prior variance. The function \\( f \\) is given a Gaussian process prior:\n\\[\nf \\sim \\mathcal{GP}(0, k(\\cdot, \\cdot)),\n\\]\nwhere \\( k \\) is a positive definite kernel on \\(\\mathcal{X}\\).\n\nThe likelihood for observed points \\(\\{x_i\\}_{i=1}^n\\) under the permanental process is (see McCullagh & M\u00f8ller, 2006; Shirai & Takahashi, 2003):\n\\[\np(\\{x_i\\}_{i=1}^n \\mid \\lambda) = \\frac{\\operatorname{per}(K)}{n!} \\exp\\left(-\\int_\\mathcal{X} \\lambda(x) dx\\right) \\prod_{i=1}^n \\lambda(x_i),\n\\]\nwhere \\(K\\) is the \\(n \\times n\\) kernel matrix with entries \\(K_{ij} = k(x_i, x_j)\\), and \\(\\operatorname{per}(K)\\) denotes the permanent of \\(K\\).\n\n**Computational Considerations and Poisson Approximation**\n\nCalculating the permanent is computationally intractable for moderate or large \\(n\\) (Valiant, 1979), as it is a #P-complete problem. In practice, for mean intensity estimation or when the focus is on first-order properties, it is common to approximate the permanental process likelihood by the Poisson process likelihood (see McCullagh & M\u00f8ller, 2006, Sec. 6). This approximation is justified when the primary interest is in the expected intensity rather than higher-order interactions, or when the kernel is such that the permanental process is close to a Poisson process (e.g., when the kernel is nearly diagonal). However, this approximation neglects the additional clustering and over-dispersion induced by the permanent structure, and thus may underestimate uncertainty or fail to capture certain dependence structures. Users should be aware of these limitations, and the full permanental likelihood should be used when feasible and when higher-order properties are of interest.\n\n*2.2 Assumptions, Identifiability, and the Diffuse Prior Limit*\n\nThe \"diffuse prior limit\" refers to the case where \\( \\tau^2 \\to \\infty \\), making the prior on \\( \\beta \\) non-informative. In this limit, the posterior for \\( \\beta \\) is determined entirely by the data, and the model allows for unconstrained estimation of the fixed effects. Identifiability of \\( \\beta \\) requires that the design matrix \\( Z \\) (with rows \\( z(x_i)^\\top \\)) has full column rank. If the covariates are collinear or nearly so, identifiability is compromised, leading to unstable or non-unique estimates of \\( \\beta \\). In practice, one should check the condition number or rank of \\( Z \\) before fitting the model. If identifiability issues are detected (e.g., via singular value decomposition or examining variance inflation factors), remedial steps include removing or combining collinear covariates, regularizing with a finite prior variance \\( \\tau^2 \\), or applying principal component analysis to the covariate matrix.\n\n*2.3 Estimation and the Representer Theorem*\n\nGiven observed data \\( \\{x_i\\}_{i=1}^n \\), estimation of the intensity function typically proceeds by maximizing a penalized log-likelihood. For the Poisson process approximation, the penalized log-likelihood is:\n\\[\n\\ell(\\beta, f) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left[ z(x_i)^\\top \\beta + f(x_i) \\right] - \\int_\\mathcal{X} \\exp\\left( z(x)^\\top \\beta + f(x) \\right) dx - \\frac{1}{2} \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k}^2.\n\\]\nHere, \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k}^2 \\) is the squared RKHS norm, which acts as a penalty term controlling the complexity of the nonparametric component \\( f \\).\n\nThe representer theorem (Kimeldorf & Wahba, 1971; Sch\u00f6lkopf et al., 2001) states that the solution \\( f^* \\) maximizing the penalized likelihood over \\( \\mathcal{H}_k \\) can be written as\n\\[\nf^*(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\alpha_i k(x, x_i),\n\\]\nfor some coefficients \\( \\alpha_i \\in \\mathbb{R} \\). Thus, the estimated intensity function decomposes into a fixed effects term and a finite linear combination of kernel functions centered at the observed data.\n\nInference can be performed via penalized maximum likelihood estimation (for point estimates) or via Bayesian posterior computation (e.g., using Laplace approximation or Markov chain Monte Carlo) when full uncertainty quantification is desired. In either case, the optimization involves both the finite-dimensional parameter \\(\\beta\\) and the coefficients \\(\\alpha_i\\) for the kernel expansion.\n\n**Computational Complexity**\n\nFor the full permanental process, evaluating the likelihood requires computing the permanent of an \\(n \\times n\\) matrix, which is computationally infeasible for \\(n > 20\\) in practice. Approximate algorithms for the permanent exist (Jerrum, Sinclair & Vigoda, 2004), but are still costly. The Poisson process approximation reduces computational complexity to \\(O(n^2)\\) for kernel evaluations and matrix operations, which is tractable for moderate \\(n\\). For large datasets, further approximations such as sparse kernel methods or inducing point approximations (Quinonero-Candela & Rasmussen, 2005) may be employed.\n\n*2.4 Equivalent Kernel: Motivation, Derivation, and Regularization*\n\n**Motivation**\n\nThe equivalent kernel characterizes how the penalized estimation procedure distributes influence from each data point across the domain, after accounting for the fixed effects. It is essential for understanding the regularization properties of the estimator and for interpreting the nonparametric component as capturing residual structure orthogonal to the fixed effects.\n\n**Derivation**\n\nLet \\( Z \\) be the \\( n \\times p \\) design matrix with rows \\( z(x_i)^\\top \\), and let \\( k(x, x') \\) be the kernel function. The RKHS norm penalizes all directions in function space, but when fixed effects are present, we wish to penalize only those directions orthogonal to the span of the covariates. The"}}